* mv_git — record-git for MultiValue.
* Copyright (C) 2026 Gordon Heydon.
*
* SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
* /**
*  * @file GIT.AGENT
*  * @version 1.0
*  */
* GIT.AGENT — an account I/O server (mv_git#47).
*
* This program does ONE thing: it reads and writes the account it is running in,
* on behalf of a process outside it.  Records, select lists, files.  It knows
* nothing about git, and deliberately so — the caller owns the git objects, and
* the only thing it cannot do for itself is reach into a UniVerse account.
*
* WHY IT EXISTS.  On UniVerse a session is the only way to records: GCI is
* licensed and dead in the Trial Edition, and InterCall is a client SDK we
* cannot get for Linux.  But that is an argument for putting the RECORD LOOP in
* a session, not the git work — and the earlier design put both there, driving
* the GIT verb from outside and shipping git objects back out to mvgitd.  This
* replaces that: uv-git already links libgit2, so it keeps the git work and asks
* a session only for what a session is for.
*
* THE PROTOCOL is the one BASIC already used to talk to mvgitd (gitproto.h),
* with the roles swapped — here the session is the SERVER.  Its constraints are
* unchanged, and each was established by probe rather than assumed:
*
*   Reads are EXACT-COUNT.  READBLK does not short-return at EOF on a FIFO:
*   OPENSEQ holds the pipe read/write, so the reader is also a writer, EOF never
*   arrives, and a read for more bytes than are present hangs.  The length header
*   is what makes every read an exact one.
*
*   Everything on the wire is HEX.  Record marks are 0xFE/0xFD/0xFC, and
*   UniVerse may have NLS or codepage translation active on sequential I/O,
*   which can alter any non-ASCII byte in transit.  OCONV "MX0C" is builtin, so
*   the encoding runs at native speed on bulk data.
*
*   Arguments are individually length-prefixed, never separated, because no byte
*   is safe as a separator inside record content.
*
* THE LICENCE is why QUIT is a real opcode rather than the caller closing a pipe.
* A session that is killed can leave its licence held; one that reaches QUIT and
* STOPs releases it.  The caller asks, the agent finishes what it is doing and
* exits cleanly — and uv-git asks even when it is itself interrupted, since it
* handles SIGINT/SIGTERM before dying.
*
* AND IT GIVES UP ON ITS OWN.  A caller that is killed outright cannot ask, and a
* caller that simply wanders off should not cost a licence either.  So the wait
* for the next request is bounded: TIMEOUT arms the read, and if nothing arrives
* within the idle limit the agent says goodbye and exits.  Verified on 14.2.1 —
* TIMEOUT does apply to READBLK on a FIFO, failing after exactly the armed
* seconds with STATUS() = 2.  That is what makes any of this possible: without it
* the agent would block forever with no EOF to wake on, since OPENSEQ holds the
* pipe read/write — the same property that forces exact-count reads everywhere
* else here.
*
* The default is 30 seconds and the caller may set it.  NOP exists for a caller
* that wants to hold a session it is not using yet: it resets the timer and does
* nothing else.  If a session does time out there is nothing to recover — the
* caller simply starts another one.
      $INCLUDE BP.INC PLATFORM.H
*     The include is back, and it costs something worth stating: the agent no
*     longer compiles in a BARE account, which is how uv-git seeds a throwaway
*     account to learn a stock VOC (mv_git#46) and how it seeds a freshly cloned
*     one (#52).  Those paths must now write a PLATFORM.H beside the agent
*     before compiling it.  The alternative — keeping the transport
*     platform-neutral — is not available: the two platforms genuinely have no
*     statement in common for an exact-count timed read.
$IFDEF MVX
*     NOT USED ON MVX, and excluded rather than merely unreached.
*
*     There is no agent here: the runtime IS the engine and reads records
*     itself, so nothing outside a session ever has to ask one for a record.
*     The body still has to COMPILE though — mkpkg bundles every BP item and
*     compiles them all — and it does not: `OPEN "DICT", NAME TO FV(NFV) ELSE`
*     is rejected by the MVX compiler, which broke the whole package build and
*     with it every MVX handler, not just this one.
      CRT "GIT.AGENT: not used on MVX — the runtime reads records directly"
      STOP
$ENDIF
$IFNDEF MVX
      AM = CHAR(254) ; VM = CHAR(253)
      MAGIC = "MVG1"
*     Header widths, mirroring gitproto.h.  Fixed-width ASCII so both ends build
*     and parse them with ordinary string operations.
      W.MAGIC = 4 ; W.TOKEN = 32 ; W.OPCODE = 16 ; W.NARGS = 2 ; W.LEN = 10
      W.STATUS = 4
      REQ.HDR = W.MAGIC + W.TOKEN + W.OPCODE + W.NARGS + W.LEN   ;* 64
*
*     The pipes and the token are the last three words of the sentence, taken
*     from the END so this works whether it is RUN or cataloged as a verb.
      S = TRIM(@SENTENCE)
      NT = DCOUNT(S, " ")
      REQP = FIELD(S, " ", NT - 3)
      RSPP = FIELD(S, " ", NT - 2)
      TOK  = FIELD(S, " ", NT - 1)
      IDLE = FIELD(S, " ", NT) + 0
      IF IDLE <= 0 THEN IDLE = 30
      IF REQP = "" OR RSPP = "" OR TOK = "" THEN
         CRT "GIT.AGENT: usage: GIT.AGENT <reqpipe> <rsppipe> <token>"
         STOP
      END
      CRT "AGENT-START req=":REQP:" rsp=":RSPP:" idle=":IDLE
*     THE TRANSPORT IS THE ONLY PLATFORM-SPECIFIC PART OF THIS PROGRAM.
*     Everything above and below is ordinary MV BASIC; these four operations —
*     open, exact-count read, write, close — are not available in the same form
*     on both platforms, so they are the seam.  See GIT.OSFILE for the same
*     shape applied to OS files.
$IFDEF UV
      OPENSEQ REQP TO RQ ELSE
         CRT "GIT.AGENT: cannot open ":REQP
         STOP
      END
      OPENSEQ RSPP TO RS ELSE
         CRT "GIT.AGENT: cannot open ":RSPP
         STOP
      END
$ENDIF
$IFDEF UDT
*     UniData BASIC has neither READBLK nor TIMEOUT — verified on 8.3, in every
*     compile mode — and the protocol needs both: exact-count reads because a
*     FIFO never reports EOF, and a deadline so an idle agent can release its
*     licence.  So the transport goes through CallC (src/agentcallc.c), which is
*     possible only because everything on this wire is hex and fixed-width
*     ASCII: CallC marshals with strlen and could not carry a byte of binary.
      AGERR = CALLC AGOPEN(REQP, RSPP)
      IF AGERR # "" THEN
         CRT "GIT.AGENT: ":AGERR
         STOP
      END
$ENDIF
      CRT "AGENT-READY"
*
*     Open file variables live in a fixed pool; the caller refers to one by its
*     slot number.  A pool rather than a name lookup because the caller may hold
*     a file and its dictionary open at once, and they share a name.
      DIM FV(64)
      FVKEYS = ""
      NFV = 0
      DONE = 0
      IDLED = 0
*
      LOOP UNTIL DONE DO
         GOSUB READREQ
         IF DONE THEN EXIT
         GOSUB DISPATCH
         GOSUB SENDRSP
      REPEAT
*     Say why we are going, before we go.  The caller may be mid-request when the
*     idle timer fires — it wrote just as we stopped reading — and a frame it can
*     recognise turns that race into "start another session" rather than a read
*     that never returns.
      IF IDLED THEN
         STATUS = 6 ; RESULT = "idle timeout"
         GOSUB SENDRSP
      END
$IFDEF UV
      CLOSESEQ RQ
      CLOSESEQ RS
$ENDIF
$IFDEF UDT
      AGERR = CALLC AGCLOSE("")
$ENDIF
      STOP
*
* ---- read one request ------------------------------------------------------
READREQ:
      HDR = ""
*     Bound the wait for the NEXT request — this is the idle timer.  An unbounded
*     read here is what would hold a licence forever behind a caller that died or
*     simply lost interest.
$IFDEF UV
      TIMEOUT RQ, IDLE
      READBLK HDR FROM RQ, REQ.HDR THEN
         NULL
      END ELSE
         IDLED = 1 ; DONE = 1 ; RETURN
      END
$ENDIF
$IFDEF UDT
*     CallC declares these parameters as `string` (udt-callc/funcs) and marshals
*     them with strlen.  REQ.HDR and IDLE are NUMBERS, and a numeric passed to a
*     string parameter does not arrive as its digits — the C side saw an empty
*     count, read nothing, and the agent exited the instant it became ready.
*     Concatenating with "" makes them strings before they cross.
      RHC = REQ.HDR : ""
      IDC = IDLE : ""
      HDR = CALLC AGREAD(RHC, IDC)
      IF HDR = "" THEN IDLED = 1 ; DONE = 1 ; RETURN
$ENDIF
      IF LEN(HDR) < REQ.HDR THEN DONE = 1 ; RETURN
      IF HDR[1, W.MAGIC] # MAGIC THEN DONE = 1 ; RETURN
      P = W.MAGIC + 1
      RTOK = HDR[P, W.TOKEN] ; P += W.TOKEN
      OP = TRIM(HDR[P, W.OPCODE]) ; P += W.OPCODE
      NARGS = HDR[P, W.NARGS] + 0 ; P += W.NARGS
      BLEN = HDR[P, W.LEN] + 0
      BODY = ""
      IF BLEN > 0 THEN
*        The body follows its header immediately, so a wait HERE is a stalled
*        transfer, not an idle session.  Armed generously and separately, so a
*        short idle limit can never truncate a large record in flight.
$IFDEF UV
         TIMEOUT RQ, 300
         READBLK BODY FROM RQ, BLEN THEN
            NULL
         END ELSE
            DONE = 1 ; RETURN
         END
$ENDIF
$IFDEF UDT
         BLC = BLEN : ""
         BODY = CALLC AGREAD(BLC, "300")
         IF BODY = "" THEN DONE = 1 ; RETURN
$ENDIF
      END
*     The token is checked on EVERY request, not just at connect.  It tells
*     apart sessions sharing one OS uid, and — the reason it is here rather than
*     at connect alone — a length-framed protocol has no resynchronisation
*     point, so once the ends disagree about where a frame starts every byte
*     after is garbage.  A checked token turns silent corruption into a clean
*     rejection.
      STATUS = 0 ; RESULT = ""
      IF RTOK # TOK THEN
         STATUS = 2 ; RESULT = "token mismatch"
         OP = ""
         RETURN
      END
*     Split the body into arguments: each is [len(10)][len hex chars].
      DIM ARGV(8)
      FOR K = 1 TO 8 ; ARGV(K) = "" ; NEXT K
*     BOFF, not OFF: OFF is a UniData keyword (its logout command) and using it
*     as a variable is a syntax error there, while UniVerse accepts it happily.
      BOFF = 1
      FOR K = 1 TO NARGS
         IF K > 8 THEN EXIT
         AL = BODY[BOFF, W.LEN] + 0 ; BOFF += W.LEN
         ENC = BODY[BOFF, AL] ; BOFF += AL
         IF AL > 0 THEN ARGV(K) = ICONV(ENC, "MX0C") ELSE ARGV(K) = ""
      NEXT K
      RETURN
*
* ---- one operation ---------------------------------------------------------
DISPATCH:
      STATUS = 0 ; RESULT = ""
      BEGIN CASE
         CASE OP = "QUIT"
            DONE = 1
         CASE OP = "PING"
            RESULT = "ok"
         CASE OP = "NOP"
*           Nothing to do, and that is exactly the point: answering resets the
*           idle timer, so a caller can hold a session it is not yet using.
            RESULT = ""
         CASE OP = "OPEN"
            GOSUB DO.OPEN
         CASE OP = "READ"
            GOSUB DO.READ
         CASE OP = "WRITE"
            GOSUB DO.WRITE
         CASE OP = "DELETE"
            GOSUB DO.DELETE
         CASE OP = "SELECT"
            GOSUB DO.SELECT
         CASE OP = "READNEXT"
            GOSUB DO.READNEXT
         CASE OP = "FILELIST"
            GOSUB DO.FILELIST
         CASE OP = "FILECLASS"
            GOSUB DO.FILECLASS
         CASE OP = "INDICES"
            GOSUB DO.INDICES
         CASE OP = "CREATEFILE"
            GOSUB DO.CREATEFILE
         CASE OP = "DELETEFILE"
            GOSUB DO.DELETEFILE
         CASE OP = ""
            STATUS = STATUS
         CASE 1
            STATUS = 3 ; RESULT = "unknown opcode: " : OP
      END CASE
      RETURN
*
*     OPEN(name [, part]) -> slot number.  `part` is "DICT" for the dictionary
*     and may be omitted for the data file — the name first, so the optional
*     argument is the one that can be left off.
DO.OPEN:
      NAME = ARGV(1) ; PART = ARGV(2)
*     ALREADY OPEN?  Hand back the same slot.
*
*     A file variable for a given file is interchangeable with any other, so
*     reopening is pure waste — and worse than waste here, because the pool is
*     finite.  The engine opens a file per INDEX ENTRY when it looks for deleted
*     records, and an account's index runs to hundreds of entries, so a pool of
*     64 was exhausted long before the scan finished.  Every open after that
*     failed, the caller read the failure as "not a record file", and deleted
*     records were silently never reported (mv_git#51).
*
*     Caching by name makes the pool a function of how many DISTINCT files an
*     account has — a handful — instead of how many records it holds.
      KEY = NAME : VM : PART
*     Format 1 (parenthesised) LOCATE only — the IN…SETTING form behaves
*     differently between flavours and must not be used in a portable port.
      LOCATE(KEY, FVKEYS; FVPOS) THEN
         RESULT = FVPOS
         RETURN
      END
      IF NFV >= 64 THEN
         STATUS = 5 ; RESULT = "too many open files"
         RETURN
      END
      NFV += 1
      IF PART = "DICT" THEN
         OPEN "DICT", NAME TO FV(NFV) ELSE
            NFV -= 1
            STATUS = 5 ; RESULT = "cannot open DICT " : NAME
            RETURN
         END
      END ELSE
         OPEN NAME TO FV(NFV) ELSE
            NFV -= 1
            STATUS = 5 ; RESULT = "cannot open " : NAME
            RETURN
         END
      END
      FVKEYS<NFV> = KEY
      RESULT = NFV
      RETURN
*
DO.READ:
      H = ARGV(1) + 0 ; ID = ARGV(2)
      IF H < 1 OR H > NFV THEN
         STATUS = 5 ; RESULT = "bad file handle"
         RETURN
      END
      READ REC FROM FV(H), ID THEN
         RESULT = REC
      END ELSE
         STATUS = 5 ; RESULT = "no record " : ID
      END
      RETURN
*
DO.WRITE:
      H = ARGV(1) + 0 ; ID = ARGV(2) ; REC = ARGV(3)
      IF H < 1 OR H > NFV THEN
         STATUS = 5 ; RESULT = "bad file handle"
         RETURN
      END
*     A bare WRITE, deliberately: UniVerse accepts `WRITE … ELSE` and UniData
*     does not, and the ELSE bought little — a write that fails raises anyway.
      WRITE REC ON FV(H), ID
      RETURN
*
DO.DELETE:
      H = ARGV(1) + 0 ; ID = ARGV(2)
      IF H < 1 OR H > NFV THEN
         STATUS = 5 ; RESULT = "bad file handle"
         RETURN
      END
      DELETE FV(H), ID
      RETURN
*
*     SELECT(handle).  The default select list is used, so exactly one scan is
*     live at a time — which is all the caller's record loop needs, and it keeps
*     the flavour-dependent "SELECT ... TO list" form out of the code.
DO.SELECT:
      H = ARGV(1) + 0
      IF H < 1 OR H > NFV THEN
         STATUS = 5 ; RESULT = "bad file handle"
         RETURN
      END
      SELECT FV(H)
      RETURN
*
*     READNEXT -> the next id, or an empty body at the end of the list.  An
*     empty result is unambiguous: a record id is never empty.
DO.READNEXT:
      READNEXT ID THEN RESULT = ID ELSE RESULT = ""
      RETURN
*
*     FILELIST -> the account's own files, "<name><VM><type>" per @AM field.
*     The same locality rules the account scan has always used: a file counts
*     when it is F or DIR and both its data and dictionary paths resolve inside
*     this account, so a Q-pointer to another account is never followed.
DO.FILELIST:
      OPEN "VOC" TO VOCF ELSE
         STATUS = 5 ; RESULT = "no VOC"
         RETURN
      END
      FILES = ""
      SELECT VOCF
      LOOP WHILE READNEXT VID DO
         READ VREC FROM VOCF, VID THEN
*           Attribute 1 is the type, but not the type ALONE: UniVerse appends
*           the description given at CREATE.FILE time, so a described file reads
*           "F Customer master" and an exact match makes it invisible.
            VT = TRIM(FIELD(VREC<1>, " ", 1))
            IF VT = "F" OR VT = "DIR" THEN
               DP = VREC<2> ; DD = VREC<3> ; LOC = 1
               IF INDEX(DP,"/",1) OR DP[1,1] = "@" THEN LOC = 0
               IF INDEX(DD,"/",1) OR DD[1,1] = "@" THEN LOC = 0
               L = LEN(VID)
               IF VID[1,1]="_" AND VID[L,1]="_" THEN LOC = 0
               IF VID[1,1]="&" AND VID[L,1]="&" THEN LOC = 0
               IF LOC THEN FILES<-1> = VID : VM : VT
            END
         END
      REPEAT
      RESULT = FILES
      RETURN
*
*     FILECLASS(name) -> the file's own CREATE.FILE parameters, as the open
*     interchange spells them: "DIR", or "hash <modulo> STATIC|DYNAMIC".
*
*     The keys are UniVerse's, and they are NOT UniData's — which is what made
*     this wrong before.  Measured on 14.2.1:
*
*       FILEINFO(f,3)  category:  4 = directory file, 1 = hashed
*       FILEINFO(f,4)  the hashed file's TYPE (17, 18, 30 …); empty for a
*                      directory
*       FILEINFO(f,5)  modulo:    1 for a directory file, the real modulo for a
*                      hashed one
*
*     The old test was UniData's "modulo <= 0 means directory".  A UniVerse
*     directory file reports modulo 1, so it never fired, every directory file
*     was recorded as hashed, and a clone recreated it as the wrong kind of file
*     entirely.
DO.FILECLASS:
      NAME = ARGV(1)
      RESULT = "hash"
      OPEN NAME TO FCV THEN
         FCAT = FILEINFO(FCV, 3)
         IF FCAT = 4 THEN
            RESULT = "DIR"
         END ELSE
            MODL = FILEINFO(FCV, 5) ; HTY = FILEINFO(FCV, 4)
            IF MODL <= 0 THEN MODL = 1
            IF HTY = 30 THEN DS = "DYNAMIC" ELSE DS = "STATIC"
            RESULT = "hash " : MODL : " " : DS
         END
      END ELSE
         STATUS = 5 ; RESULT = "cannot open " : NAME
      END
      RETURN
*
*     CREATEFILE(name, class).  `class` is the open interchange form: "DIR", or
*     "hash", or "hash <modulo> DYNAMIC|STATIC" carrying the source file's real
*     geometry so a clone rebuilds the file at its true size.
*
*     CREATE.FILE is INTERACTIVE and there is no non-interactive form of it that
*     can be trusted.  Probed on 14.2.1: `CREATE.FILE X 1,2 1,2 TYPE 19` reports
*     success, creates a Type 18 file — not the directory asked for — and puts
*     "TYPE 19" in the VOC record as the file's DESCRIPTION.  A trailing token it
*     does not understand becomes a description rather than an error, which is
*     the same trap that once hid whole files from the account scan (mv_git#43).
*     So the prompts are answered properly, with DATA stacking the replies:
*     dictionary modulo/separation/type, the same three for the data part, then
*     the description — which must be EMPTY, or the VOC type reads "F <text>"
*     and code matching attribute 1 against "F" cannot see the file.
DO.CREATEFILE:
      NAME = ARGV(1) ; CLS = TRIM(ARGV(2))
      IF NAME = "" THEN
         STATUS = 5 ; RESULT = "no file name"
         RETURN
      END
*     Already there?  Say so and stop: a second CREATE.FILE asks whether to
*     recreate, and an unanswered prompt would hang the session.
      OPEN NAME TO XV THEN
         RESULT = "exists"
         RETURN
      END
      KIND = FIELD(CLS, " ", 1)
      MODL = FIELD(CLS, " ", 2) + 0
      DYN  = TRIM(FIELD(CLS, " ", 3))
      IF MODL <= 0 THEN MODL = 1
      IF KIND = "DIR" THEN
         DTYPE = 19 ; MODL = 1
      END ELSE
*        18 is an ordinary static hashed file, and it is what this path can
*        create.  Type 30 (dynamic) is REJECTED when answered into these
*        prompts — verified on 14.2.1: CREATE.FILE re-asks and the stacked
*        answers run out, leaving a half-built file with a dictionary and no
*        data part.  So a file the commit calls dynamic is recreated static
*        here rather than not at all; resizing it afterwards is a decision for
*        whoever knows the data, and losing the file is not.
         DTYPE = 18
      END
      DATA 1, 2, 3, MODL, 2, DTYPE, ""
      EXECUTE "CREATE.FILE " : NAME CAPTURING CFOUT
      OPEN NAME TO XV2 THEN
         RESULT = "created"
      END ELSE
         STATUS = 5
         RESULT = "CREATE.FILE " : NAME : " did not produce a usable file: " : CFOUT[1,200]
      END
      RETURN
*
*     DELETEFILE(name).  DELETE.FILE asks for confirmation, so the answer is
*     stacked the same way — an unanswered prompt would hang the session, and a
*     hung session holds a licence.
DO.DELETEFILE:
      NAME = ARGV(1)
      IF NAME = "" THEN
         STATUS = 5 ; RESULT = "no file name"
         RETURN
      END
      OPEN NAME TO XV3 ELSE
         RESULT = "absent"
         RETURN
      END
      DATA "Y", "Y"
      EXECUTE "DELETE.FILE " : NAME CAPTURING DFOUT
      OPEN NAME TO XV4 THEN
         STATUS = 5
         RESULT = "DELETE.FILE " : NAME : " left the file in place: " : DFOUT[1,200]
      END ELSE
         RESULT = "deleted"
      END
      RETURN
*
DO.INDICES:
      NAME = ARGV(1)
      RESULT = ""
      OPEN NAME TO IXV THEN
         IXL = INDICES(IXV)
         IF IXL # "" THEN RESULT = IXL
      END ELSE
         STATUS = 5 ; RESULT = "cannot open " : NAME
      END
      RETURN
*
* ---- send one reply --------------------------------------------------------
SENDRSP:
      ENC = ""
      IF RESULT # "" THEN ENC = OCONV(RESULT, "MX0C")
      EL = LEN(ENC)
      PADV = STATUS ; PADW = W.STATUS ; GOSUB ZPAD ; SS = PADR
      PADV = EL     ; PADW = W.LEN    ; GOSUB ZPAD ; SL = PADR
      SH = MAGIC : SS : SL
$IFDEF UV
      WRITEBLK SH TO RS ELSE DONE = 1 ; RETURN
      IF EL > 0 THEN
         WRITEBLK ENC TO RS ELSE DONE = 1 ; RETURN
      END
$ENDIF
$IFDEF UDT
      AGERR = CALLC AGWRITE(SH)
      IF AGERR # "" THEN DONE = 1 ; RETURN
      IF EL > 0 THEN
         AGERR = CALLC AGWRITE(ENC)
         IF AGERR # "" THEN DONE = 1 ; RETURN
      END
$ENDIF
      RETURN
*
* ---- a fixed-width, zero-filled number -------------------------------------
* Every length and status on this wire is fixed width, and FMT is the obvious
* way to write one.  It is also wrong: the "%" zero-fill code is honoured on
* UniVerse and IGNORED on UniData, which returns the MASK ITSELF —
*     FMT(5, "R%4")  ->  "0005"  on UniVerse
*                    ->  "%4"    on UniData 8.3
* so the reply header went out as "MVG1%4%10" — 9 characters where the reader
* was waiting for 18.  Nothing reported an error: the far end simply blocked
* until the session died, and a protocol that cannot resynchronise has no way
* to say "that frame was malformed".  Pad by hand instead.  It is three lines,
* and it is three lines that mean the same thing everywhere.
ZPAD:
      PADR = PADV : ""
      IF LEN(PADR) < PADW THEN PADR = STR("0", PADW - LEN(PADR)) : PADR
      RETURN
$ENDIF
